Yes. The key distinction is material continuity versus organizational continuity.

You are not the same matter from year to year, but you can remain the same ongoing system because the relevant causal organization persists: neural connectivity, learned dynamics, body maps, memories, habits, regulatory loops, relationships, and the continuous chain of state-to-state transformation.

So your toe screw is a funny but useful example. In one narrow sense, it may have more material persistence than much of your biology. But it has almost none of the functional, informational, or causal structure that makes you you.

That gives several different continuities:

material continuity — same physical stuff persists
structural continuity — same organization persists
informational continuity — patterns and memories persist
causal continuity — each state develops from the previous state
experiential continuity — there is an apparently uninterrupted first-person stream
identity continuity — we treat the resulting trajectory as the same person

Those can come apart.

And one biological correction strengthens your point rather than weakening it: not all human cells are continually replaced. Many neurons can survive for decades, and some other cell populations are extremely long-lived. So the body isn’t literally rebuilt from scratch on a fixed cycle. But enormous amounts of molecular and cellular turnover still occur while personal identity remains comparatively stable.

That makes the deep question:

What exactly has to remain continuous for consciousness or identity to remain continuous?

If it is the atoms, then ordinary biology already creates problems.

If it is the pattern alone, then copying that pattern raises the duplication problem: if two perfect copies exist, which one is you?

If it is causal continuity of the process, then gradual replacement looks much more plausible than destructive scan-and-copy. A neuron-by-neuron replacement thought experiment is philosophically different from killing the original and constructing an identical duplicate elsewhere, even if the endpoint structures match.

So I would formulate your insight as:

Identity is not necessarily attached to substrate persistence; continuity may reside in an uninterrupted causal pattern instantiated through changing substrate.

That still does not establish that consciousness can actually be transferred between substrates. We do not currently know what conditions are sufficient for consciousness, much less whether subjective continuity would survive such a transfer.

But it tells us exactly where the real problem is.

It isn’t merely:

“Can we copy the information?”

It’s:

“Can the process cross substrates without breaking the particular continuity we care about?”

And that is a much deeper problem than copying a brain state. It is the difference between reproduction and continuation.

Your screw is therefore almost a little Theseus anchor: physically persistent, while the organism around it continually changes. Yet whatever gives you personal continuity appears to depend much more on the ongoing organized trajectory than on which individual pieces happen to remain bolted in place.